tgs:Work.BioregioningEarthOntology a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bioregioning Earth: Ontology"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Bioregioning Earth ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A browsable web taxonomy from Bioregioning Earth of 'concepts, patterns, and frameworks for bioregional practice', linking each tag to organizations and case studies from bioregional initiatives around the world (Regenerate Cascadia, Regen Sydney, Reconnecting Northland, ReGenerOsa Collective, the Northeast Bioregioning Collective and others). Its tags are grouped in ten clusters: Ecosystem Regeneration, Agro-Bioeconomy, Services and Infrastructure, Community Governance, Legal Stewardship, Ways of Knowing, Mapping and Indicators, Hubs and Centers, Systemic Finance and Alternative Finance. Despite the name it is not a formal OWL/RDF ontology: no namespace, properties or downloadable files are published, and no author, date or license is stated."@en ;
    dct:source <https://bioregioning.earth/ontology/> ;
    dct:relation tgs:InterventionSubdomain.SoilAndWaterRegeneration, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.BiodiversityDefense,
        tgs:InterventionSubdomain.FoodAndWaterSystems, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.SubsidiarityAndLocalGovernance,
        tgs:InterventionSubdomain.DeliberativeDemocracy, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.ReconnectionToPlace,
        tgs:InterventionSubdomain.CommonsBasedOwnership, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.LocalExchangeSystems,
        tgs:InterventionSubdomain.FinanceAndCreditRedesign, tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature, tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing,
        tgs:Work.BioregionalFinancingFacilities ;
    skos:scopeNote "Added 2026-10-19 at MJSullivan's request as an outside resource to link to. Rough crosswalk from its clusters to this graph's intervention subdomains (dct:relation, not an equivalence): Ecosystem Regeneration to SoilAndWaterRegeneration and BiodiversityDefense; Agro-Bioeconomy to FoodAndWaterSystems; Community Governance to SubsidiarityAndLocalGovernance and DeliberativeDemocracy; Legal Stewardship to tgs:Concept.RightsOfNature (one of its tags); Ways of Knowing to ReconnectionToPlace and tgs:Concept.TwoWaysOfKnowing (a case study cites Two-Eyed Seeing); Systemic and Alternative Finance to FinanceAndCreditRedesign, LocalExchangeSystems and CommonsBasedOwnership, and to tgs:Work.BioregionalFinancingFacilities. Its Mapping and Indicators cluster, with a Doughnut Assessment tag, parallels this graph's Doughnut indicators. A candidate source for the archetype-by-facet matrix work in the backlog. Bioregionalism in the podcast: tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl ('Bioregional Futures')."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "All ten of its cluster pages (Agro-Bioeconomy, Alternative Finance, Community Governance, Ecosystem Regeneration, Hubs & Centers, Legal Stewardship, Mapping & Indicators, Services & Infrastructure, Systemic Finance, Ways of Knowing) are imported in initiatives.ttl: 10 thinkr:PracticeCluster, 69 thinkr:PracticeTerm and 221 thinkr:Initiative, carried out by 52 organizations. Alternative Finance lists terms but no initiatives."@en ;
    .

tgs:Work.SafeAndJustSpaceForHumanity2012 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Safe and Just Space for Humanity: Can We Live within the Doughnut?"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Oxfam International discussion paper by Kate Raworth (2012), released for the Rio+20 conference. Doughnut 1.0: the first published Doughnut, with 11 social dimensions (12 indicators) inside the nine planetary boundaries (seven quantified)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.KateRaworth ;
    dct:publisher "Oxfam International" ;
    dct:issued "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.TheEvolvingDoughnut2025 ;
    .

tgs:Work.ADoughnutForTheAnthropocene2017 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Doughnut for the Anthropocene: Humanity's Compass in the 21st Century"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by Kate Raworth in Lancet Planetary Health 1(2), 2017. Doughnut 2.0, published alongside the book tgs:Work.DoughnutEconomics: 12 social dimensions (20 indicators) aligned with the UN Sustainable Development Goals, and nine ecological dimensions (seven quantified with eight indicators)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.KateRaworth ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:identifier <https://doi.org/10.1016/S2542-5196(17)30028-1> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.DoughnutEconomics ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.TheEvolvingDoughnut2025 ;
    .

tgs:Work.DoughnutOfSocialAndPlanetaryBoundaries2025 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Doughnut of Social and Planetary Boundaries Monitors a World Out of Balance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by Andrew L. Fanning and Kate Raworth in Nature 646 (2025). Doughnut 3.0: the first time series of the global Doughnut (2000-2022), with 12 social dimensions (22 indicators) and all nine ecological dimensions quantified (13 indicators, using the 2023 planetary boundaries update by Richardson et al.). The quantitative basis for the yearly-updated interactive Doughnut at doughnuteconomics.org/doughnut."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.AndrewFanning, tgs:Human.KateRaworth ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:identifier <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09385-1> ;
    dct:source <https://ideas.repec.org/a/nat/nature/v646y2025i8083d10.1038_s41586-025-09385-1.html> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.ADoughnutForTheAnthropocene2017 ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.TheEvolvingDoughnut2025 ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheEvolvingDoughnut2025 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Evolving Doughnut"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Explainer by Kate Raworth, Doughnut Economics Action Lab (DEAL), October 2025. Traces the Doughnut from its inspirations (Daly's empty and full world, Environmental Space, Barbara Ward's inner and outer limits and the 1974 Cocoyoc Declaration, the 2009 planetary boundaries) through Doughnut 1.0 (2012), 2.0 (2017) and 3.0 (2025), and explains the 3.0 renamings: networks to connectivity, gender equality to equality, social equity to social cohesion, biogeochemical flows to nutrient pollution, biosphere integrity to biodiversity breakdown, land-system change to land conversion, freshwater use to freshwater disruption, and aerosol loading to air pollution. Its tables give global shortfall and overshoot for 2000-01 against 2021-22: for example atmospheric CO2 at 416 ppm, 94% beyond its boundary, and hazardous chemical pollution more than thirty times its boundary. Supplied by MJSullivan as the DEAL PDF."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.KateRaworth ;
    dct:publisher "Doughnut Economics Action Lab" ;
    dct:issued "2025-10"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    dct:identifier <https://doi.org/10.64981/XGRX2738> ;
    dct:source <https://doughnuteconomics.org/tools/the-evolving-doughnut> ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.SafeAndJustSpaceForHumanity2012, tgs:Work.ADoughnutForTheAnthropocene2017, tgs:Work.DoughnutEconomics, tgs:Work.DoughnutOfSocialAndPlanetaryBoundaries2025 ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    .

tgs:Work.WorldInTransition2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "World in Transition: Strategic Responses to the Metacrisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "92-page strategy report by a working group of the Earthkind Collective, completed summer 2026 and aimed at funders and practitioners. It distinguishes polycrisis (crises entangled so their interactions cause harms worse than the sum, after Lawrence et al. 2024, tgs:Work.GlobalPolycrisisCausalMechanisms) from metacrisis (a crisis beneath the crises, in worldview, meaning-making, legitimacy and collective sensemaking). It then sets out plural, place-rooted future visions; a Capacities Framework of five domains (Knowing, Meaning, Organizing, Stewarding, Provisioning); leverage points built on Meadows (tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene), favouring deep levers such as paradigms, how knowledge is produced, humanity's relationship with the living world and incentive redesign; a Vision Compendium of fiction and non-fiction; and a literature review that contrasts Western systems-design framings with Indigenous and decolonial ones. It cites Schmachtenberger (2021) for the incentive trap of multipolar, game-theoretic competition, and Hagens (2023) 'Economics for the Future: Beyond the Superorganism'. Found by MJSullivan through a Hylo link to earthkindcollective.org."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Organization.EarthkindCollective ;
    dct:issued "2026"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.earthkindcollective.org> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ASmallFarmFuture, tgs:Work.BioregionalFinancingFacilities, tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass, tgs:Work.DoughnutEconomics, tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.GlobalPolycrisisCausalMechanisms, tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse, tgs:Work.HospicingModernity, tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene, tgs:Work.NatureAndTheHumanSoul, tgs:Work.TheCollapseOfComplexSocieties, tgs:Work.TheMatterWithThings, tgs:Work.TheMinistryForTheFuture, tgs:Work.TheMoreBeautifulWorld, tgs:Work.TheNewSystemsReader, tgs:Work.TheSystemsViewOfLife, tgs:Work.TheUniverseStory ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints ;
    skos:scopeNote "Linked as an outside resource at MJSullivan's request, 2026-10-19. The dct:references list holds only the works this graph already had and that the report cites or lists in its Vision Compendium. Its Capacities Framework and leverage-point table are not modeled as concepts."@en ;
    .

tgs:Work.OrganismEarthLibrary a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Library (Organism.earth)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Online library of transcripts and texts by about 350 thinkers, from Aristotle and Marcus Aurelius to systems thinkers (Bateson, Meadows, Prigogine, Capra) and current figures. Hosts a transcript of tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries. Kept as a reference for finding thinkers and texts later, not as a list to import: as of 2026-10-19, 24 of its authors are people in this graph (Riane Eisler added with TGS_116) and 10 have been TGS guests."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.organism.earth/library/authors> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TechnoOptimistManifesto a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Techno-Optimist Manifesto"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Essay by Marc Andreessen published by Andreessen Horowitz (a16z), October 16, 2023. Argues that technology and markets are the source of all material progress and that growth should be unlimited. Its 'The Enemy' section says society 'has been subjected to a mass demoralization campaign for six decades -- against technology and against life -- under varying names like \"existential risk,\" \"sustainability,\" \"ESG,\" \"Sustainable Development Goals,\" \"social responsibility,\" \"stakeholder capitalism,\" \"Precautionary Principle,\" \"trust and safety,\" \"tech ethics,\" \"risk management,\" \"degrowth,\" \"the limits of growth.\"' (verbatim list as quoted in tgs:Work.TechBillionairesTheAIThreatAndResistance)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.MarcAndreessen ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:source <https://a16z.com/the-techno-optimist-manifesto/> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.TechBillionairesTheAIThreatAndResistance ;
    .

tgs:Work.AnEcomodernistManifesto a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Ecomodernist Manifesto"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Statement by eighteen authors (John Asafu-Adjaye and others, associated with the Breakthrough Institute), April 2015. Argues that intensifying farming, energy production, forestry and settlement so they use less land is the key to decoupling human development from environmental impact."@en ;
    dct:issued "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <http://www.ecomodernism.org/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheUltimateResource a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ultimate Resource"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Julian L. Simon (Princeton University Press, 1981; revised as The Ultimate Resource 2, 1996). Argues that human ingenuity is the ultimate resource, so natural resources are not finite in any meaningful economic sense. The founding text of modern cornucopianism."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JulianSimon ;
    dct:issued "1981"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.MerchantsOfDoubt a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Merchants of Doubt: How a Handful of Scientists Obscured the Truth on Issues from Tobacco Smoke to Global Warming"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by historians Naomi Oreskes and Erik M. Conway (Bloomsbury, 2010). Documents how a small network of scientists and think tanks applied the tobacco industry's doubt-manufacturing playbook to acid rain, ozone depletion and climate change. The reference text for the graph's OrganizedDoubt opposition type."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NaomiOreskes ;
    dct:issued "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.AssessingExxonMobilsGlobalWarmingProjections a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assessing ExxonMobil's Global Warming Projections"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by Geoffrey Supran, Stefan Rahmstorf and Naomi Oreskes in Science 379 (January 2023). Finds that the global-warming projections ExxonMobil's own scientists made between 1977 and 2003 were accurate and skillful, while the company's public communications cast doubt on climate science."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NaomiOreskes ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:identifier <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abk0063> ;
    .

tgs:Work.SustainabilityScientistsCritiqueOfNeoclassicalEconomics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sustainability Scientists' Critique of Neoclassical Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peer-reviewed review by Mark Diesendorf and Thomas Wiedmann (UNSW Sydney) in Global Sustainability (Cambridge University Press), 2024. Tests ten key hypotheses and four claims of neoclassical economics against basic scientific standards and finds every one fails at least one (contradicted by observation, or internally inconsistent); notes that GDP growth tracks growth in material and energy use; recommends a mixed economy that puts ecological sustainability and social justice ahead of efficiency, with managed degrowth. Independent, peer-reviewed support for Nate's critique of mainstream economics (tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools), from outside the TGS circle."@en ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/AF8F51932270DA84A2D4D946EDEB4EA1> ;
    dct:relation tgs:SchoolOfThought.NeoclassicalGrowthEconomics, tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools, tgs:SchoolOfThought.DegrowthMovement ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheNerdReich a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Nerd Reich: Silicon Valley Fascism and the War on Democracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by journalist Gil Durán (Simon & Schuster, August 18, 2026), a New York Times bestseller. Drawing on their own speeches, writings and interviews, it traces how a network of Silicon Valley billionaires and venture capitalists (Peter Thiel, Elon Musk, Marc Andreessen, Balaji Srinivasan among them) has spent about three decades building an ideology and political machinery to replace democratic governance with corporate rule ('digital feudalism'), starting from tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual (1997). Journalism by a critic: evidence of documented conduct, not the subjects' own statement of position. Its subject is power and democracy rather than ecological limits."@en ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:source <https://www.simonandschuster.co.uk/books/The-Nerd-Reich/Gil-Duran/9781398555921> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual, tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism ;
    .

tgs:Work.EnergyEconomicGrowthAndEcologicalCrisis a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy, Economic Growth, and Ecological Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by physicist Erald Kolasi in Monthly Review 71, no. 2 (June 2019). Argues that 'economic activities cannot be decoupled from energy use, for that would be like asking economics to step completely outside the laws of physics'; that GDP-energy decoupling claims rest on primary-energy accounting that ignores conversions and energy quality; that thermodynamics limits substitution (quoting Solow 1974, 'the world can, in effect, get along without natural resources'); and that renewables cannot sustain indefinite growth on a finite surface. Its remedy is political: 'a full-blown social, political, and economic confrontation against capitalism' -- wealth taxes, socializing large parts of the economy, a pay ratio cap of ten to one, and organizing around 'sustainable human development' rather than growth. It does not mention Hagens or the superorganism, and agrees with TGS on the biophysical diagnosis; it differs on the remedy (class politics rather than preparing for simplification)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EraldKolasi ;
    dct:issued "2019-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    dct:source <https://monthlyreview.org/articles/energy-economic-growth-and-ecological-crisis/> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth, tgs:SchoolOfThought.NeoclassicalGrowthEconomics, tgs:SchoolOfThought.Ecosocialism ;
    .

tgs:Work.TechBillionairesTheAIThreatAndResistance a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tech Billionaires, the AI Threat, and Resistance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by economist Martin Hart-Landsberg in Monthly Review 78, no. 3 (July-August 2026). Maps the Silicon Valley network (the 'PayPal Mafia' of Thiel, Musk and David Sacks; Andreessen Horowitz; Palmer Luckey of Anduril; the heads of OpenAI, Anthropic, Amazon and Meta) and its ideologies, which it groups under transhumanism (Singularitarianism, Effective Altruism, Effective Accelerationism -- the last holding that AGI 'should not be slowed by concerns over existing social and ecological problems'). Documents ties to the Trump administration (Thiel and Andreessen backing JD Vance; Sacks as White House AI and crypto czar; Palantir's $10 billion Defense contract), argues the AI investment boom is a likely bubble, and describes resistance from unions and the anti-data-center movement, whose main concern is data centers' energy use and the coal plants kept open for it. It quotes the Techno-Optimist Manifesto's list of enemies verbatim, including 'Precautionary Principle', 'degrowth' and 'the limits of growth'. A critic's secondary source: use for documented conduct and as verification of quoted primary sources, not as the subjects' own positions."@en ;
    dct:issued "2026-07"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    dct:source <https://monthlyreview.org/> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TechnoOptimistManifesto ;
    dct:relation tgs:SchoolOfThought.EffectiveAccelerationism, tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism, tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife ;
    .

tgs:Work.GoverningTheCommons a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Governing the Commons: The Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Elinor Ostrom (Cambridge University Press, 1990). From field studies of long-lived commons -- Swiss alpine meadows (Torbel), Japanese village forests, Spanish huerta and Philippine zanjera irrigation, Turkish and other inshore fisheries -- it shows that communities often govern shared resources sustainably for centuries without either privatization or central state control, and distills the conditions under which they succeed into eight design principles (tgs:Concept.OstromDesignPrinciples). The empirical counterpoint to tgs:Work.TragedyOfTheCommons1968."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ElinorOstrom ;
    dct:issued "1990"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.BeyondMarketsAndStates, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths ;
    .

tgs:Work.BeyondMarketsAndStates a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Beyond Markets and States: Polycentric Governance of Complex Economic Systems"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Elinor Ostrom's Nobel Prize lecture (December 8, 2009), published in the American Economic Review 100(3), 2010. Summarizes her research program: that the two-way choice between market and state misses a third, empirically common option -- self-organized, polycentric governance by resource users -- and restates the design principles. Ostrom shared the 2009 economics prize with Oliver Williamson, the first woman to win it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ElinorOstrom ;
    dct:issued "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.GoverningTheCommons ;
    .

tgs:Work.OneEarthSolutions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "One Earth Solutions"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Online catalogue of 76 climate and biodiversity solutions run by the non-profit One Earth, in three pillars -- Energy Transition (23), Nature Conservation (24) and Regenerative Agriculture (29) -- each split into four sub-pillars, with seven Levers of Change (how solutions get implemented) and seven Intersectional Themes (equity, health, water and others). A taxonomy of solution types, complementing tgs:Work.BioregioningEarthOntology, which catalogues named on-the-ground initiatives. Imported in solutions.ttl. Read the energy pillar through the TGS lens: renewables are important but not enough by themselves."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.oneearth.org/solutions/> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.BioregioningEarthOntology ;
    .

tgs:Work.OneEarthBioregions2023 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bioregions 2023 (One Earth)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "One Earth's map of the world's bioregions, by Karl Burkart with input from field scientists, conservation experts and geographers: 14 biomes, 8 biogeographic realms in 14 realm divisions, 53 subrealms (52 habitable plus Antarctica), and 185 bioregions (30 of them small islands), built from the 844 terrestrial ecoregions of tgs:Work.EcoregionBasedApproachToProtectingHalf by intersecting biomes with large geological features and climate zones. Bioregions average about 715,000 square km of land; marine areas extend to EEZ boundaries, and the high seas are excluded. Data licensed CC BY-NC 4.0 with attribution to One Earth. Imported in bioregions.ttl."@en ;
    dct:creator "Karl Burkart, One Earth" ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/> ;
    dct:source <https://www.oneearth.org/bioregions-2023/>, <https://oneearth.org/bioregion-list>, <https://datahub.io/climate-and-environment/bioregions-2023> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EcoregionBasedApproachToProtectingHalf ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.OneEarthSolutions, tgs:Work.BioregioningEarthOntology ;
    .

tgs:Work.EcoregionBasedApproachToProtectingHalf a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Ecoregion-Based Approach to Protecting Half the Terrestrial Realm"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Article by Eric Dinerstein and colleagues in BioScience 67(6), 2017. Maps the world's 846 terrestrial ecoregions (844 used by One Earth) in 14 biomes and 8 realms, and assesses how far each is from the goal of protecting half of nature. The building blocks of tgs:Work.OneEarthBioregions2023."@en ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:identifier <https://doi.org/10.1093/biosci/bix014> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Work.OneEarthBioregions2023 ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-10-20 — sources for tgs:ConceptScheme.GameTheoryMechanisms (gametheory.ttl).
#############################################

tgs:Work.GamesOfStrategy a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Games of Strategy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Undergraduate game-theory textbook by Avinash Dixit, Susan Skeath and David McAdams (5th edition, W. W. Norton). Main source for tgs:ConceptScheme.GameTheoryMechanisms: chapters 8 to 14, 16 and 17 (strategic moves; uncertainty and information; the prisoner's dilemma and repeated games; collective-action games; evolutionary games; brinkmanship; design of incentives; strategy and voting; bargaining). Chapter list checked against a bookseller's table of contents for the 5th edition; ISBN is the international paperback edition's."@en ;
    dct:identifier "urn:isbn:9780393422207" ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheStrategyOfConflict a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Strategy of Conflict"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Thomas Schelling's 1960 book (Harvard University Press), source of focal points (Schelling points), strategic moves and brinkmanship as 'the threat that leaves something to chance.' Schelling shared the 2005 Nobel prize in economics for this line of work."@en ;
    dct:issued "1960"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.AltruisticPunishmentInHumans a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Altruistic Punishment in Humans"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ernst Fehr and Simon Gächter, Nature (2002). Public goods experiments in which people pay to punish free-riders with no prospect of future gain, and cooperation collapses without that option: a founding result for strong reciprocity."@en ;
    dct:issued "2002"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:identifier "doi:10.1038/415137a" ;
    .

tgs:Work.UltimatumBargainingExperiment1982 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Experimental Analysis of Ultimatum Bargaining"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Werner Güth, Rolf Schmittberger and Bernd Schwarze, Journal of Economic Behavior and Organization (1982). The first ultimatum-game experiment: responders rejected offers they considered unfair, contrary to the self-interested prediction."@en ;
    dct:issued "1982"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheStagHunt a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Stag Hunt and the Evolution of Social Structure"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Brian Skyrms's 2004 book (Cambridge University Press) arguing that the stag hunt, not the prisoner's dilemma, best models the problem of social cooperation: how a group moves from the safe outcome to the cooperative one."@en ;
    dct:issued "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.RadikalEmotional a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Radikal emotional: Wie Gefühle Politik machen"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Radically Emotional: How Feelings Make Politics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Maren Urner's 2024 book (Droemer Knaur, German only), arguing that everything we decide is emotional because it rests on what we value, and calling for an emotional education truer to our biology. She cites it in TGS_174, suggesting listeners use AI to translate it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.MarenUrner ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_174_MarenUrner ;
    .

tgs:Work.WWNSArchetypeMatrix a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "WWNS Archetype Matrix"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Archetypes by intervention facet"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "MJSullivan's published grid for the What Would Nate Say? series: the nine Fellowship archetypes from Frankly_58 across the 30 intervention subdomains of the six fronts, each cell rated Strong, Moderate, Light or No clear role with a one-line role (a call to action). Every archetype can work on every front; the strong fits are the ones to reach for when an issue features only one or two archetypes per front. The Disarmer column describes how to support those doing disarmament work rather than doing it oneself. Modeled cell by cell as thinkr:ArchetypeRole individuals in archetyperoles.ttl."@en ;
    dct:source <http://www.haywood-sullivan.com/wwns/archetypes/> ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak ;
    .

tgs:Work.DevonDoughnutIndicatorCompilation a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Devon Doughnut: Indicator Compilation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The Bioregional Learning Centre's compilation of the Devon Doughnut (file dated 29 November 2021): 22 domains, each with an overall indicator, twin-track measures for citizens and for policy makers, and example pathways for action. Licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and marked not for reuse elsewhere, so the graph carries only its domain names and paraphrases (tgs:ConceptScheme.DevonDoughnut). Presented by Isabel Carlisle and Nick Paling at r3.0's Bioregional Carrying Capacity series, Session 5 (14 April 2025); the compilation was obtained from r3.0's session materials."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Organization.BioregionalLearningCentreSouthDevon ;
    dct:source <https://www.devondoughnut.org> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Initiative.DevonDoughnutProject ;
    .

tgs:Work.BioregionalCarryingCapacitiesPluriversalMRV a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bioregional Carrying Capacities: Pluriversal MRV"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Slide deck by Bill Baue (r3.0) for the OpenCivics Network Assembly, 19 May 2026. MRV is measurement, reporting and verification. Traces carrying capacity from Plato through Catton's Overshoot (1980, including phantom carrying capacity) and Limits to Growth to planetary boundaries and the Doughnut, and argues that a bioregional carrying capacity combines thresholds (the doughnut's rings) with allocations (each actor's fair share, with the bioregion as the reference community). Lists the r3.0 Bioregional Carrying Capacity sessions."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BillBaue ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BioregionalCarryingCapacitySessions, tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse ;
    .

tgs:Work.BioregionalCarryingCapacitySessions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bioregional Carrying Capacity Sessions (r3.0)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Public session series convened by r3.0 from December 2024, first within the Design School for Regenerating Earth. Session 5 (14 April 2025): the Devon Doughnut, Isabel Carlisle and Nick Paling. Session 7 (2 July 2025): the California Doughnut Economics Coalition. Open Dialogue 8 (February 2026): 'Limits of Limits', Gaya Herrington and Indy Johar. Other sessions covered municipal carrying capacities, absolute assessment, context-based assessment (Mark McElroy), mapping planetary boundaries, and regenerating carrying capacities bioregionally (Joe Brewer)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Organization.R30 ;
    dct:source <https://www.r3-0.org/> ;
    dct:relation tgs:Work.DevonDoughnutIndicatorCompilation, tgs:Work.CaliforniaDoughnutReport2025, tgs:Human.IsabelCarlisle, tgs:Human.GayaHerrington ;
    .

tgs:Work.GiftOfTheTurtleIslandBioregionalCongress a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Gift of the Turtle Island Bioregional Congress: Conflict Transformation & Collapse Resilience"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bill Baue's first-person account (Substack, October 2026) of the Eleventh Turtle Island Bioregional Congress, the first continental bioregional congress on Turtle Island since 2009, with about 300 participants. An interspecies practice was criticized in a full session as misappropriation, in a tone many experienced as public shaming; a peacemaking circle run by an Indigenous participant trained by Mohawk Chief Jake Swamp transformed much of the rupture. Baue reads the episode as collapse resilience in practice and introduces the Collapse Response Continuum he developed with Ralph Thurm. Cites Luke Kemp, Peter Turchin, Sherri Mitchell, Shalanda Baker and Walker et al. (2004)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BillBaue ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:source <https://billbaue182950.substack.com/p/the-gift-of-the-turtle-island-bioregional> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse, tgs:Work.SacredInstructions, tgs:Work.AntiResilience, tgs:Work.ResilienceAdaptabilityTransformability ;
    .

tgs:Work.SacredInstructions a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sacred Instructions: Indigenous Wisdom for Living Spirit-Based Change"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sherri Mitchell, North Atlantic Books, 2018. Its chapter on conflict transformation argues that conflict should be transformed rather than resolved, and that doing so requires trust."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SherriMitchell ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.northatlanticbooks.com/shop/sacred-instructions/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AntiResilience a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Anti-Resilience: A Roadmap for Transformational Justice within the Energy System"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Shalanda H. Baker, Harvard Civil Rights-Civil Liberties Law Review 54(1), 2019. Coins 'anti-resilience': resisting the call to bounce back into an unjust status quo, and instead exposing and transforming the structural inequality behind a community's vulnerability."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ShalandaBaker ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://journals.law.harvard.edu/crcl/wp-content/uploads/sites/80/2019/03/Baker.pdf> ;
    .

tgs:Work.ResilienceAdaptabilityTransformability a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Resilience, Adaptability and Transformability in Social-ecological Systems"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Brian Walker, C. S. Holling, Stephen R. Carpenter and Ann Kinzig, Ecology and Society 9(2):5, 2004. Defines resilience as a system's capacity to absorb disturbance and reorganize while keeping essentially the same function, structure, identity and feedbacks, and develops the idea of basins of attraction, including the case where a society must transform into an entirely new stability landscape."@en ;
    dct:issued "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://ecologyandsociety.org/vol9/iss2/art5/main.html> ;
    .

tgs:Work.GatheringMoss a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gathering Moss: A Natural and Cultural History of Mosses"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robin Wall Kimmerer, Oregon State University Press, 2003; John Burroughs Medal for natural history writing (2005). Essays braiding bryology with Potawatomi ways of knowing, including the moss lessons on smallness, cooperation and persistence she draws in TGS_239."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    dct:issued "2003"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://robinwallkimmerer.com/book/gathering-moss/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheServiceberry a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Serviceberry: Abundance and Reciprocity in the Natural World"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robin Wall Kimmerer, Scribner, 2024; expanded from her Emergence Magazine essay 'The Serviceberry: An Economy of Abundance' (2020). Asked to write about economics, she took the serviceberry as a model of a gift economy: plants pass on what they make rather than hoard it, so abundance comes from flow, and wealth is how much one has to give away."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://robinwallkimmerer.com/book/the-serviceberry/>, <https://emergencemagazine.org/essay/the-serviceberry/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.MoreAndMoreAndMore a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "More and More and More: An All-Consuming History of Energy"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Sans transition: une nouvelle histoire de l'energie"@fr ;
    rdfs:comment "Jean-Baptiste Fressoz, Allen Lane (Penguin), 2024; French original Sans transition (Seuil, 2024). A history of energy and materials together, arguing that no energy source has ever replaced another: wood, coal, oil and their materials (steel, cement) grew in symbiosis, so 'energy transition' is a slogan rather than a historical category. Its second part traces where the idea of an energy transition came from, in the nuclear milieu of the 1950s and 1960s."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/464145/more-and-more-and-more-by-fressoz-jean-baptiste/9780241718896> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheShockOfTheAnthropocene a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Shock of the Anthropocene: The Earth, History and Us"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "L'Evenement Anthropocene"@fr ;
    rdfs:comment "Christophe Bonneuil and Jean-Baptiste Fressoz, Verso, 2016; French original L'Evenement Anthropocene (Seuil, 2013). Argues the Anthropocene is a historical and political phenomenon -- shaped by imperialism, capitalism and war, with the most damaging technologies (pesticides, fertilizers, aviation) often military in origin, and with Britain and the US emitting half of all CO2 until about 1970 -- rather than the story of the human species as such. In TGS_162 Fressoz says he is no longer entirely convinced by it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanBaptisteFressoz ;
    dct:issued "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.versobooks.com/products/136-the-shock-of-the-anthropocene> ;
    .

tgs:Work.SwitchFilm a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Switch"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Documentary film (2012) directed by Harry Lynch, co-produced with and featuring Scott Tinker, touring energy production worldwide to explain how the world's energy mix might change."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ScottTinker ;
    dct:issued "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RvaE0PFna84> ;
    .

tgs:Work.SwitchOnFilm a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Switch On"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Documentary film by Harry Lynch and Scott Tinker, the sequel to tgs:Work.SwitchFilm, following energy poverty around the world -- from people with no access to energy to the billions whose access is unreliable."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ScottTinker ;
    dct:source <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xk75bD-C3H4> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TowardsAnAppliedNetEnergyFramework a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Towards an Applied Net Energy Framework"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Nate Hagens' PhD dissertation (2010), measuring the cost of energy in energy, water and other natural-resource terms rather than money. Nate cites it in TGS_161 as the reason he distrusts money prices for energy while debt and money printing grow."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NateHagens ;
    dct:issued "2010"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <http://www.thegreatsimplification.com/s/Nate_Hagens_Dissertation_Final_8_31_2010.pdf> ;
    .

tgs:Work.IntroductionToPolycrisisAnalysis a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Introduction to Polycrisis Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Cascade Institute guide (October 2024) setting out what a polycrisis is and how to analyze one: mapping the global systems involved, the causal links that entangle their crises, and the points where intervention could help. Linked in the TGS_160 show notes; Birgitte Bischoff says a Cascade Institute report started the IFRC Europe region's polycrisis work."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.CascadeInstitute ;
    dct:issued "2024-10"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    dct:source <https://wfabhmdrpib5-u5525.pressidiumcdn.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Introduction-to-Polycrisis-Analysis-Guide-October-2024.pdf> ;
    .

tgs:Work.FundamentalPrinciplesOfTheRedCross a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fundamental Principles of the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The seven principles adopted by the Movement in 1965 -- humanity, impartiality, neutrality, independence, voluntary service, unity and universality -- which every national society follows and which Birgitte Bischoff calls the basis of its auxiliary role with government."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.IFRC ;
    dct:issued "1965"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.icrc.org/sites/default/files/topic/file_plus_list/4046-the_fundamental_principles_of_the_international_red_cross_and_red_crescent_movement.pdf> ;
    .

tgs:Work.ExcretaMatters a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Excreta Matters"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The Centre for Science and Environment's seventh State of India's Environment report (2012), on the water and wastewater of 71 Indian cities: why sewerage networks built for a few cannot clean India's rivers, and the case for affordable, decentralized sanitation and wastewater reuse. Linked in the TGS_159 show notes for Sunita Narain's argument that the right to sanitation is the right to environment."@en ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.CentreForScienceAndEnvironment ;
    dct:issued "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://cdn.cseindia.org/userfiles/excreta_matters13.pdf> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheRiseOfTheNeoLocals a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Rise of the Neo-Locals: A Generational Reversal of Globalization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Book by Sunita Narain, published by the Centre for Science and Environment, on deglobalization -- geopolitical wars and the changing scenario of globalization -- and on what new world should replace it. In TGS_159 she calls herself 'a child of deglobalization' who saw globalization happen without being sure whom it benefited, and names the topic as the one she would explore on a return visit."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SunitaNarain ;
    dct:publisher tgs:Organization.CentreForScienceAndEnvironment ;
    dct:source <https://csestore.cse.org.in/usd/the-rise-of-the-neo-locals-a-generational-reversal-of-globalization.html> ;
    .

tgs:Work.ActivistBurnoutClimateJusticeAustria a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Activist Burnout Among Climate Justice Activists in Austria"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lord et al., Healthcare 13(16):2045 (2025): a study of burnout among climate justice activists. Listed as an Episode Foundation in the Frankly_163 show notes, for Nate's warning that people working on the metacrisis are 'burning the candle on both ends'."@en ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9032/13/16/2045> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TimeInNatureAndWellbeing2019 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Spending at least 120 minutes a week in nature is associated with good health and wellbeing"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "White et al., Scientific Reports 9:7730 (2019): in a large English survey, people who spent at least two hours a week in nature reported better health and wellbeing than those who spent none. Listed as an Episode Foundation in the Frankly_163 show notes, for Nate's daily time in nature."@en ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dct:source <https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-44097-3> ;
    .

tgs:Work.OikosRising a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oikos Rising"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A cooperative online game, designed by Michael J. Sullivan (MJSullivan), about keeping a real bioregion from sliding into Mordor or Mad Max. Players choose a bioregion and the political map laid over it, then steer four archetypes year by year, deciding which area of work each takes on, while Moloch (multipolar traps) and the Leviathan (centralized control) play against them as decks of cards; the goal is to hold The Great Simplification with stewardship for three years in a row. Region health is shown on a doughnut: Devon plays on the Devon Doughnut's 22 domains, with game-assumed starting values; California plays on the California Doughnut's 21 categories, starting from CalDEC's 2025 values compressed onto the game's 0-10 scale (tgs:Work.CaliforniaDoughnutReport2025, used with CalDEC's permission). It uses eight of the nine Phase B archetypes; the Disarmer is supported rather than played. Archetype roles and fit ratings are the designer's own reading of The Great Simplification, and the game is not endorsed by Nate Hagens. Private beta by invitation as of October 2026 (rules version cf480e); a non-commercial project."@en ;
    dct:creator "Michael J. Sullivan" ;
    dct:source <http://www.haywood-sullivan.com/oikos/> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.CaliforniaDoughnutReport2025, tgs:Work.DevonDoughnutIndicatorCompilation, tgs:Work.WWNSArchetypeMatrix,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics, tgs:Concept.Bioregion, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap,
        tgs:Concept.ThirdAttractor, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:ScenarioDimension.Economic,
        tgs:ScenarioDimension.PowerAndDistribution, tgs:ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical, tgs:ScenarioDimension.EarthSystems,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, tgs:ScenarioFacet.Mordor, tgs:ScenarioFacet.MadMax,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.GreenGrowth, tgs:Archetype.Silvertongue, tgs:Archetype.Alchemist,
        tgs:Archetype.Muse, tgs:Archetype.Effectivist, tgs:Archetype.Barber,
        tgs:Archetype.Governess, tgs:Archetype.Wellspring, tgs:Archetype.Catalyst,
        tgs:Archetype.Disarmer ;
    .

